  "reason": "Ticket sender reports Excel shows Unlicensed Product and editing is blocked. Checking account status and devices per KB-026."
}

{
  "user_id": 9,
  "reason": "Confirm user is signing in successfully with company account (KB-026 quick check) and whether any sign-in failures or prompt loops appear."
}

{
  "hostname": "BL-LT-0109",
  "reason": "Check device state for Office activation issue; low disk space can affect Office updates (KB-028/KB-017)."
}

{
  "outcome": "escalated",
  "reply_to_user": "Hi Mei, thanks for reporting this. Your account is active and signing in normally, so the problem looks like the Excel license on your laptop rather than your password. I've passed it to our second-level team, who can check the license assignment. You don't need to do anything else for now. If Excel stops working completely before they reach out, reply to this ticket and let us know.",
  "internal_note": "Checked lookup_user (user_id 9, Mei Chen, Operations, active, not blocked, 0 failed logins, password set 2026-09-11, MFA working). check_signin_logs: last 10 sign-ins all successful via m365 (corporate network) and VPN (residential), Duo push, no failures, no MFA denials, all within usual hours from Chicago. check_device_status BL-LT-0109: macOS 15, online, checked in 2026-10-11, 75 GB free (14.6%), no warnings. Per KB-026 'Escalate when', the user signs in correctly but still sees Unlicensed product, so this is likely a missing Microsoft 365 license assignment. I could not verify licensing with my tools. Escalating to Tier 2 to confirm and assign the license. Notes for Tier 2: the laptop is macOS, so the KB-026 Windows account-removal step does not apply. Mailbox is at 99.4% of quota (50.9 GB of 51.2 GB), which may cause editing or sync problems and should be checked alongside the license. No password, lockout, or sign-in action was taken.",
  "kb_articles": [
    "KB-026",
    "KB-028"
  ],
  "confidence": "high"
}
